.Dd August 9, 2026
.Dt USBIPD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm usbipd
.Nd export local USB devices over the network
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl 4 | Fl 6
.Op Fl t Ar port
.Op Fl a
.Ar busid ...
.Nm
.Fl l
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon hands local USB devices to remote machines using the USB/IP
protocol, so that a device plugged in here can be used as though it
were plugged in there.
The remote end may be
.Fx
running
.Xr vhci 4 ,
or Linux, or any other client that speaks the protocol.
.Pp
It runs entirely in userland, on top of
.Xr libusb 3
and
.Xr ugen 4 ,
so nothing needs to be loaded or configured in the kernel to export a
device.
It does need to run as root, because it opens the device.
.Pp
A device is exported only if it is named on the command line.
That is deliberate: an imported device is taken over completely by the
client, so exporting one by accident means losing the use of it here.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-t port"
.It Fl l
List the local USB devices and exit.
The first column is the bus id to pass to this command, and to
.Nm usbip Cm attach Fl b
on the client.
.It Fl a
Export every device rather than only those named.
Convenient on a machine dedicated to the purpose; unwise anywhere else.
.It Fl t Ar port
Listen on this TCP port instead of the default 3240.
.It Fl 4 , Fl 6
Listen on IPv4 or IPv6 only.
The default accepts both.
.It Fl v
Report connections and exports on standard error.
.El
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
Bus ids are the names
.Fx
already uses, as printed by
.Xr usbconfig 8 ,
so
.Ar ugen0.2
rather than the port path Linux would show.
.Pp
While a device is exported, its interfaces are claimed and its
.Fx
drivers cannot use it.
They come back when the client detaches or the connection drops.
.Pp
Clients are served concurrently, so one machine holding a device does
not stop others from listing what is available or importing something
else.
A device itself goes to one client at a time; a second attempt to
import the same one is refused.
.Pp
Transfers are carried asynchronously, so a client may have many
outstanding at once and an endpoint that never answers does not block
the others.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Export one device and watch what happens:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# usbipd -l
Local USB devices:
  ugen0.2        0781:5567  High Speed (480 Mbit/s)
                  if0  class 08/06/50
# usbipd -v ugen0.2
.Ed
.Pp
From the client:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# usbip attach -r thishost -b ugen0.2
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libusb 3 ,
.Xr ugen 4 ,
.Xr vhci 4 ,
.Xr usbconfig 8 ,
.Xr usbip 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon was written in 2026.
It speaks the same protocol as the Linux daemon of the same name but
shares no code with it.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Atsushi Furuta Aq Mt furuta@furuta.bsdclub.org
.Sh CAVEATS
The connection is neither encrypted nor authenticated, and anyone who
can reach the port can take the exported device and everything on it.
Export only onto a network you trust.
.Sh BUGS
Isochronous transfers are not carried; a submission for one is refused
rather than ignored, so a client sees an error instead of waiting.
Audio interfaces and webcams will not work.
